\description{
Computes Kleinberg's HITS hub and authority scores over the same
cleaned, redirect/canonical-folded, domain-filtered, deduplicated link
graph that \code{\link[=pagerank]{pagerank()}} builds, so node identities line up across the two
centrality measures. Wraps \code{igraph::hits_scores()} (the non-deprecated
successor of \code{igraph::hub_score()} / \code{igraph::authority_score()}).
}
\details{
\subsection{Relationship to the PageRank pipeline}{

\code{hits()} reuses the exact identity-forming steps of \code{\link[=pagerank]{pagerank()}} — URL
canonicalization (the same resolved \code{rurl} profile), the same composed
redirect + canonical fold map, the same domain/host filtering, the same
\code{duplicate_edge_policy} deduplication, and the same self-loop / isolate
handling. The resulting vertex set therefore matches \code{pagerank()} run with
the same arguments, so hub, authority, and PageRank can be joined on
\code{node_name} without re-canonicalizing.

The PageRank-specific, \emph{forward-flow} modeling devices have \strong{no HITS
analog and are intentionally not exposed}: nofollow evaporation, the
indexability (noindex / robots.txt) transforms, the TIPR teleport prior, and
the \code{reverse} flag. HITS already computes both directions of authority flow
(hub is the outflow-oriented score, authority the inflow-oriented one), so a
separate reversal is unnecessary.
}

\subsection{Matrix formulation and the whole-graph caveat}{

With adjacency matrix \eqn{A}, \strong{authority} is the dominant eigenvector of
\eqn{A^\top A} ("pages pointed to by pages that point to many things") and
\strong{hub} is the dominant eigenvector of \eqn{A A^\top} ("pages that point to
pages pointed to by many things"). See \code{\link[=compute_hits]{compute_hits()}}.

Kleinberg's original HITS (1999) was run on a small, \strong{query-focused base
set} of pages, where the hub/authority distinction is sharply
interpretable. \code{hits()} instead runs on the \strong{full (or user-filtered) site
graph} that \code{pagerankr} assembles. The eigenvector computation is identical
and correct, but the interpretation shifts: scores describe hub/authority
structure across the whole crawled graph rather than relevance to a specific
query. Treat them as site-wide structural centralities, not query-relevance
scores.
}
}
